# Fix `react-hooks/exhaustive-deps`

Don't reach for `// eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps` — that rule catches a real class of bug (stale closures, effects that stop reacting to a prop they read). There are three legitimate fixes, in order of preference. Pick based on what the missing dependency actually is.

## Option 1: Add the dependency (default; do this unless you have a reason not to)

Open the effect and look at which identifier the rule flagged. If the effect reads it and the value can legitimately change over the component's life, add it to the array:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  fetchUser(userId).then(setUser);
}, [userId]); // ← added
```

If the flagged value is a **function or object defined inline in the component body**, adding it directly usually causes an infinite loop (new identity every render). That means the real problem is the inline definition, not the missing dep — go to Option 2 or 3.

## Option 2: Stabilize the value so it's a legitimate dependency

If the effect reads a callback or object that should not change every render, wrap it so its identity is stable, then add it:

```tsx
const handleSave = useCallback((data: FormData) => {
  api.save(data);
}, []);          // stable identity

useEffect(() => {
  register(handleSave);
  return () => unregister(handleSave);
}, [handleSave]); // ← now safe to list
```

Same idea for objects passed to the effect — build them with `useMemo`, or destructure the primitives out and depend on those instead:

```tsx
// instead of depending on `options` (new object every render):
useEffect(() => {
  doThing(options.timeout, options.retries);
}, [options.timeout, options.retries]); // ← depend on primitives
```

## Option 3: Escape hatch — only when the value is provably stable

If the value truly never changes but React can't prove it (a module-level constant, a `useRef().current`, a stable `dispatch` from a reducer), move it **outside** the component body so it's not reactive at all — then the rule stops flagging it:

```tsx
// module scope — not reactive, no dep needed
const LOG_ENDPOINT = "/api/log";

function Widget() {
  useEffect(() => {
    fetch(LOG_ENDPOINT);
  }, []); // LOG_ENDPOINT is not a component-scope binding
}
```

Only if the value genuinely must live in component scope and genuinely cannot change (e.g. a ref captured deliberately) is a scoped disable the right call — and even then, put it on the specific line with a comment explaining why, not a blanket disable:

```tsx
// intentionally reading latest ref value once on mount; ref.current is stable here
// eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps
useEffect(() => { ... }, []);
```

That last form should be rare. If you're reaching for it on the first attempt, you've almost certainly got an unstable value you should be stabilizing instead.

## Verify

After the fix:

```bash
npx eslint src/ --ext .ts,.tsx       # hooks rule clean, no new disables
npx tsc --noEmit                     # types still check
npm test                             # no behavioral regression
npm run build                        # the failing build step now passes
```

If the effect's behavior actually changed (firing more or fewer times), confirm that's what you want — that's exactly what the rule was protecting against.

